  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the brake wheel?",
      "a": "The brake wheel is the big gear on the windshaft. A wooden brake band around its rim can be tightened to stop the sails, which is how it got its name.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you stop a windmill?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the wallower do?",
      "a": "The wallower is the first gear on the upright shaft. Meshing with the brake wheel at an angle, it turns the sails' motion down the length of the mill.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the motion get down the mill?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a stone nut?",
      "a": "A stone nut is a small gear driven by the great spur wheel. Because it is much smaller, it spins fast and turns a millstone.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do millstones turn faster than the sails?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a windmill grind flour?",
      "a": "Grain is fed through a hole in the turning runner stone. Grooves on the stones cut and grind it as it moves outward, and flour spills from the edge.",
      "asks": [
        "How do millstones work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do windmills turn to face the wind?",
      "a": "The sails work best facing straight into the wind. Many mills have a fantail, a small fan at the back, that turns the cap automatically when the wind changes.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a fantail on a windmill?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Windmills were grinding grain in Persia by the 9th century and in Europe by the late 12th century.",
    "The fantail, which turns a mill's cap into the wind automatically, was patented by Edmund Lee in 1745."
  ],
